course: biology
lesson: 275
title: Fossils: how a living thing turns to stone and how we dig it up
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A fossil is a living thing, or a mark it made, that was kept in rock: a shell, a bone, a leaf, a footprint, even a dinosaur's poo.
explain: Picture a shell sinking into soft mud at the bottom of the sea; more mud piles on top, and over millions of years water full of dissolved minerals seeps through and slowly fills or replaces the shell with stone.
explain: Most dead animals never get that chance, because they are eaten, rot or are smashed up, so a fossil is a lucky accident that needs a quick burial away from air.
explain: That is why the curly stone you pick up on a beach is so special: it is the shape of a sea animal that died long before the first person walked the Earth.
explain: Scientists who study fossils are called palaeontologists, and they dig very slowly with brushes and small picks, because a careless hit can destroy a find that waited millions of years.
fact: A fossil is the shape of a living thing turned into stone or pressed into rock; most fossils are of sea animals with hard parts like shells and bones.
fact: A shell or bone becomes a fossil when it is buried in mud or sand and minerals carried by water slowly fill or replace it.
fact: Ammonites are curly fossil shells of sea animals related to squid and octopus; they died out at the same time as the big dinosaurs, about 66 million years ago.
fact: In Whitby in England, people once carved snake heads on ammonites and sold them as snakes turned to stone.
fact: Far fewer than one in a million animals becomes a fossil, because most bodies are eaten, rot or are broken up; only a quick burial without air gives a chance.
fact: Trace fossils are marks animals left behind, such as footprints, burrows, tooth marks, nests, eggs and fossil poo; they show how animals walked, hid and lived.
fact: Palaeontologists dig slowly, map where each bone lies, and wrap big bones in plaster jackets, like a cast on a broken arm, to carry them safely.
fact: Cleaning one big skeleton out of hard rock can take years of patient work, because the rock can be harder than the bone itself.
fact: In many places loose fossils on a beach may be collected, but digging into cliffs is dangerous and often forbidden, because rocks fall and tides can trap people.
fact: Children have found important fossils, such as a young T. rex found by boys in North Dakota in 2022; they told scientists, and the find went to a museum.
fact: Some fossils catch a moment, such as a fish with another fish in its mouth, because fast burial froze the scene.
fact: Petrified wood is wood turned to stone as minerals replaced it cell by cell; the Petrified Forest in Arizona is full of it, and tree rings can still be seen.
fact: Some historians think ancient people who found Protoceratops skulls in the Gobi Desert imagined griffins, and that mammoth skulls with one big nose hole may have inspired stories of one-eyed giants; this is an idea, not something proven.
fact: Lucy is a 3.2-million-year-old skeleton of a human relative found in Ethiopia in 1974; her hips and knees show she walked upright, and she was named after a Beatles song playing at the camp.
exercise: What is a fossil? | The shape of a living thing turned into stone or pressed into rock | Any stone at all that has a pretty pattern or a nice colour on it | A living animal that has simply grown very old, stiff and hard | A rock that fell from space
exercise: Which kind of animal makes most of the fossils we find? | Sea animals with hard parts like shells | Birds with soft feathers and hollow bones | Jellyfish and worms | Butterflies and moths with big wings
exercise: How does a buried shell slowly turn to stone? | Minerals carried by water fill or replace it | The Sun bakes it hard on the beach | Ice freezes it solid for ever | Lightning strikes it and melts it
exercise: A curly stone shaped like a snail is often an ammonite. What was an ammonite? | A sea animal related to squid and octopus | A land snail that grew very big in the forests | A coiled snake turned to stone | A shell worn round and smooth by the waves
exercise: Why do so few animals become fossils? | Most bodies are eaten, rot or are broken up before they are buried | Only very old animals can turn into fossils, never young ones or babies | Animals must die in a desert to become fossils | Fossils can form only during earthquakes
exercise: Which of these is a trace fossil? | A dinosaur footprint in rock | A dinosaur's thigh bone in rock | An ammonite shell | A shark's tooth
exercise: Why do palaeontologists wrap big bones in plaster jackets? | To protect them while they are carried, like a cast on a broken arm | To make them look whiter and cleaner when they go on show in the museum | To keep the bones warm in winter | To stop the bones from growing
exercise: Why can cleaning one skeleton take years? | The rock can be harder than the bone | Scientists are only allowed to work one day a year | The bones keep growing back into the rock | Museums ask them to work slowly on purpose
exercise: Why is digging fossils out of a sea cliff a bad idea? | Rocks can fall and the tide can trap you | Fossils in cliffs are poisonous to touch | Cliff fossils turn to dust in sunlight | Cliffs are always private gardens
exercise: Some boys in North Dakota found a young T. rex in 2022. What did they do? | They told scientists, and it went to a museum | They kept it hidden in their garage for many years | They sold the bones at a car boot sale for cash | They glued the bones together themselves at home
exercise: What can a fossil of a fish with another fish in its mouth tell us? | What the fish ate and how it hunted | That fish could talk to each other long ago | That all fossils are fake | That fish lived on dry land then
exercise: What is petrified wood? | Wood turned to stone as minerals replaced it cell by cell | Wood so frightened by a storm that it went stiff for ever | Wood that was burnt into black charcoal | Plastic made to look like wood
exercise: Which animal's skulls may have inspired the story of the griffin? | Protoceratops | Stegosaurus | Tyrannosaurus rex | The dodo bird of Mauritius
exercise: Where was Lucy, a 3.2-million-year-old human relative, found? | In Ethiopia | In northern England | In Australia | In southern Brazil
exercise: What do Lucy's hips and knees show? | She walked upright on two legs | She swung through trees by her arms only | She walked on all four legs like a dog | She could swim very fast in rivers
exercise: Where did Lucy's name come from? | A Beatles song playing at the camp | The name of the finder's dog | The country where she was found | A famous queen of Egypt
test: «found a curly stone on the beach, my nan says it's a snake turned into stone» What is right? | It is most likely an ammonite, a sea animal with a coiled shell | Yes, snakes curl up tight and then turn to stone when they die | It is a snail that died last summer | It is a stone polished by waves, never alive
test: «mate reckons every dinosaur that ever lived turned into a fossil, that's why museums have so many» What is right? | No, far fewer than one in a million animals becomes a fossil | Yes, every bone turns to stone in the end if you wait long enough | Yes, but only the big ones | No, none of them did, fossils are all plaster
test: What would happen to a dead fish lying on the seabed for years without being buried? | It would most likely be eaten or rot away and leave no fossil | It would turn to stone in a few weeks, as all dead fish do in the sea | It would float up and become a cloud | It would grow into a new fish
test: Count them: a museum shows 3 fossil footprints of a two-legged dinosaur, each with 3 toes. How many toe marks are there in all? | 9 | 6 | 12 | 3
test: «found a massive bone sticking out of the cliff, gonna dig it out with dad's hammer» What is best? | Leave it, keep away from the cliff and tell a museum | Dig fast before the tide comes in and washes it away | Hit it hard so it breaks out in one go | Take only the end that sticks out
test: In a cartoon, a dog digs in the garden and finds a whole shiny dinosaur skeleton in one afternoon. What is closer to the truth? | Real fossils are usually broken and stuck in rock, and digging one out takes months or years | Real dinosaurs are buried in gardens just under the grass | Real fossil bones are always white and shiny | Any dog can smell fossil bones under rock
test: «poo can't be a fossil lol» What is right? | It can: fossil poo is a trace fossil and shows what animals ate | Right, only hard bones and shells can ever be fossils, never poo | Only bird poo can be a fossil | Fossil poo is just a joke in films
test: A man in the 1800s cuts a log of petrified wood and sees rings. What do the rings tell him? | The stone was once a real tree that grew year by year | The stone was carved by people to look like wood | The rings were made by waves on a beach | The tree turned to stone in one year
test: «cyclops were real, they found the skulls» What is closest to the truth? | Some historians think mammoth skulls with one big nose hole inspired stories of one-eyed giants | Yes, scientists have dug up real cyclops skeletons | Mammoths really had only one eye | The skulls were of giant owls
test: «Lucy's just a monkey, right» What is right? | No, she was an early human relative who walked upright | Yes, she was a chimp that lived in a zoo in Africa | She was a modern woman found in 1974 | She was a dinosaur named after a song
test: Count them: a palaeontologist wraps 4 big bones in plaster jackets and 2 more wait for tomorrow. How many big bones need jackets in all? | 6 | 4 | 8 | 2
exam: Identify the principal condition which renders the fossilisation of an organism more probable. | Rapid burial in sediment, in the absence of air | Prolonged exposure to sunlight upon the surface | Consumption of the remains by scavengers | Death at an advanced age
exam: Which of the following is correctly classified as a trace fossil? | A preserved burrow or footprint | A mineralised thigh bone | A petrified tree trunk | An ammonite shell
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the collection of fossils from coastal cliffs. | Excavation of cliffs is hazardous and frequently prohibited, whereas loose specimens may in many places be collected | Excavation of cliffs is universally encouraged by authorities | Fossils may lawfully be removed only by persons under ten years of age | All fossils upon beaches are the property of the finder without exception
exam: State the significance of the hips and knees of the specimen designated Lucy. | They demonstrate that the individual walked upright | They demonstrate that the individual was a reptile, not a primate | They demonstrate that the individual could fly short distances | They demonstrate that the individual lived in the sea
